<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wenceslaus I, Duke of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 907–935</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Saint Wenceslas, Good King Wenceslas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wenceslaus I, known as Saint Wenceslas, was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from about 921. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and accepted the overlordship of the German king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>. He was murdered in 935, or possibly 929, by his brother Boleslaus I, and was soon venerated as a martyr. He became the patron saint of the Czech lands and the hero of the English carol Good King Wenceslas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Wenceslaus I, Duke of Bohemia" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wolfenb%C3%BCttelsk%C3%BD_kodex_%E2%80%93_Gumpoldova_legenda_%E2%80%93_18v_cr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
